The training set shows that the highest retention ratios are associated with two families of compositions: (i) Ni-base alloys containing high Ni (23–36 wt%), Ti, Al, B and W (targets 0.82–1.00) and (ii) a single low-alloy Cr–Mo steel that is compositionally almost identical to one pool member (target 0.937).  

Nearest-neighbour matching of every candidate against these high-target examples immediately isolates five compositions:

- c019 is the closest analogue of the two Ni–W–Ti–Al alloys that recorded 1.00 and 0.875;  
- c015 differs from the 0.937 Cr–Mo steel by only a few tenths of a percent in Ni, Cr, C and Si;  
- c011 reproduces the high-Ti, high-Ni, Al–B alloy that recorded 0.903;  
- c013 and c004 sit next to the Ni–Ti–Al–W alloy that recorded 0.821 (c013 additionally carries W + Mo).  

All remaining candidates lie near training points whose targets are 0.40–0.70 and are therefore ranked lower.  

Final: c019, c015, c011, c013, c004
